fc7a3a6c729e7409c201d8a85af464ba516fafa1c3f40bdd8b7ce0"},"seconds":8.66536259651184,"seed":123798290605175096,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to find the length of the other leg of the second right triangle, given that both triangles have the same area.\n\nFirst, we calculate the area of the first right triangle. The area \\(A\\) of a right triangle is given by the formula:\n\\[ A = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} \\]\n\nFor the first triangle, the base is 8 cm and the height is 5 cm. So, the area is:\n\\[ A = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 8 \\times 5 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 40 = 20 \\text{ cm}^2 \\]\n\nSince the second triangle has the same area, its area is also 20 cm\\(^2\\). Let the length of the other leg of the second triangle be \\(x\\) cm. The second triangle is a right triangle with one leg of 20 cm and the other leg of \\(x\\) cm. Therefore, its area is:\n\\[ A = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 20 \\times x = 10x \\text{ cm}^2 \\]\n\nWe know that the area of the second triangle is 20 cm\\(^2\\), so we set up the equation:\n\\[ 10x = 20 \\]\n\nTo find \\(x\\), we solve the equation by dividing both sides by 10:\n\\[ x = \\frac{20}{10} = 2 \\]\n\nThus, the length of the other leg of the second triangle is \\(\\boxed{2}\\) cm."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"prealgebra:367"}
